new file mode 100644
index 0000000..591e8fe
--- /dev/null
+++ b/compat-tests/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,80 @@
+# LOCAL EXPERIMENT — NOT A RELEASE CANDIDATE
+
+Base: Evolution `2.3.7`, commit `cd800f2976e1e5b682fbf86a01ee4d85ae61f370`.
+The user has not approved a production Evolution change. No deployment or live
+WhatsApp traffic was attempted. This branch is a review artifact only.
+
+The critical advisory GHSA-qvv5-jq5g-4cgg affects the deployed Baileys rc9.
+The upstream v7 fix starts at rc12; rc13 also fixes the `fromMe` regression in
+rc12. This branch evaluates rc13; it does not establish its safety or Evolution
+compatibility. See https://github.com/WhiskeySockets/Baileys/security/advisories/GHSA-qvv5-jq5g-4cgg
+and https://github.com/WhiskeySockets/Baileys/releases/tag/v7.0.0-rc13.
+
+Separate finding: the published `Curve.verify` wrapper in both the pinned rc9
+image (libsignal 2.0.1) and npm rc13 (libsignal 6.0.0) ignores the boolean
+returned by `verifySignature`. Synthetic tests showed false at the underlying
+function and true at the wrapper for an altered message/signature or wrong
+key. In rc9 a call site is pairing `validate-connection`; in rc13 call sites
+also include Noise certificate checks. This does not prove exploitability or
+impact in the deployed environment. An independent review is required.
+
+`scripts/patch-baileys-curve.cjs` is a *candidate*, not an upstream fix: it
+replaces that wrapper with `return verifySignature(...) === true`, failing
+closed for non-true values. It checks exact Baileys and libsignal versions,
+plus the sha256 of the unpatched/patched wrapper, and hashes both libsignal
+`curve.js` and `session_builder.js` resolved from Baileys, including nested
+installations. Unknown bytes/versions fail. The Dockerfile copies the script
+and applies it after `npm ci`; npm build and `npm run start:prod` check that
+the patched rc13 wrapper is present. The rc9 path is permitted only in the
+offline patch test and is refused by the release check. Direct `node dist/main`
+or other commands bypassing the npm lifecycle still skip the check; mutation
+after checking is also not prevented. These are release blockers. The published npm
+package supplies precompiled JS, so editing a TypeScript source file in this
+Evolution checkout would not change the installed module; patching the installed
+module before bundling/runtime is required for this candidate. A maintained
+upstream release or reviewed fork is preferable before any production rollout.
+
+**Important unresolved security issue:** in both examined libsignal versions,
+`session_builder.js` calls `verifySignature(..., true)`, and the underlying
+function returns true when `isInit` is true. The caller does not check the
+return value. This candidate does not change that behavior; its implications
+and compatibility need independent investigation. The candidate is not a
+comprehensive security fix and does not establish absence of other CVEs.
+
+Local observations on Node 24 Linux:
+
+* `npm install --package-lock-only --ignore-scripts --save-exact baileys@7.0.0-rc13`
+  changed 64 installed package entries, notably libsignal 2.0.1 → 6.0.0,
+  Jimp/media dependencies, and a new `whatsapp-rust-bridge` dependency.
+* `npm ci --ignore-scripts` passed using this lockfile. Prisma client was
+  generated locally with a synthetic localhost URI and `npm run build`
+  passed (TypeScript, CJS and ESM). A local Node 24 Alpine Docker build with
+  the environment proxy CA mounted temporarily reached and passed `npm ci`
+  with lifecycle scripts, then was intentionally stopped as shared disk space
+  dropped to 3.8 GiB. Its final image, service startup and migrations are not
+  validated. The first build attempt failed Alpine TLS verification before the
+  CA was supplied; verification was not disabled.
+* `node --test compat-tests/patch-guard.test.cjs` checks refusal of missing or
+  modified patch, wrong package versions and changed bytes in a temporary tree.
+  It also exercises the actual candidate entrypoint with fake commands and
+  verifies a failed guard prevents migrations and start.
+* `node compat-tests/crypto-offline.mjs` checks known synthetic HKDF/MD5,
+  cross-version ECDH/signatures and rejection of changed message, signature,
+  length and key. It expects an independently installed rc9 fixture at
+  `/workspace/voice-poc-deps/node_modules/baileys` (override with
+  `VOICE_POC_BASELINE`). That npm rc9 fixture resolved libsignal 6.0.0, so it
+  alone is not proof of the production rc9 dependency set.
+* `compat-tests/rc9-image-offline.mjs` ran in the exact pinned image with
+  `--network none --read-only` and ephemeral `/tmp`; it copied the package to
+  tmpfs, patched that copy and verified valid and invalid synthetic signatures
+  against image libsignal 2.0.1. It made no changes to the image or deployment.
+* `compat-tests/baileys-send-offline.mjs` exercises rc13's real message socket
+  to a fake `sendNode` with synthetic identity/media and no network. Empty token
+  storage triggers an extra background privacy IQ attempt, intercepted by the
+  fixture; a prepopulated synthetic token suppresses it. There is no real media
+  upload, Signal session initialization, handshake, webhook or delivery test.
+
+These checks do not validate the actual installed/reconnected WhatsApp sessions,
+message histories, event provenance, group behavior, full encryption protocol,
+operational migration/rollback or concurrent Evolution deployments. Production
+tests and independent security review remain necessary before a release.
