new file mode 100644
index 0000000..bd15365
--- /dev/null
+++ b/compat-tests/SECURITY_ASSESSMENT.md
@@ -0,0 +1,79 @@
+# Bounded security assessment — local review only
+
+As of 2026-11-03 this is not an approved production change. Findings are
+independent and must not be merged or described as one verified exploit.
+
+## 1. Upstream advisory (confirmed affected dependency)
+
+Baileys upstream classifies [GHSA-qvv5-jq5g-4cgg / CVE-2026-48063](https://github.com/WhiskeySockets/Baileys/security/advisories/GHSA-qvv5-jq5g-4cgg)
+as critical and describes risks involving forged events, history and app-state
+corruption. Upstream lists fixes 6.7.22 and 7.0.0-rc12. Its [rc13 release](https://github.com/WhiskeySockets/Baileys/releases/tag/v7.0.0-rc13)
+also fixes a `fromMe` regression introduced in rc12. Railway was observed to
+use image digest `sha256:1bd8afc4a6cf48822e6cf02469aeae7bd35a12a6b616eacd1291926307f4d339`;
+local inspection of that same immutable image reported Baileys `7.0.0-rc.9`.
+The dependency is in the affected range. No production exploitation or actual
+impact has been established; filtering a field or switching history sync off
+is not a complete workaround according to upstream.
+
+## 2. Local signature observations (not part of that advisory)
+
+Local tests of the pinned image rc9/libsignal 2.0.1 and npm rc13/libsignal 6.0.0
+showed that `Curve.verify` returns true on synthetically modified messages,
+signatures and wrong keys although the underlying library returns false. It
+discards that boolean. Known call sites are pairing validation in rc9 and
+pairing plus Noise certificate verification in rc13. The candidate returns
+only an actual true result and rejects invalid cases in both local fixtures.
+No network traffic, real credentials or exploitation test was used.
+
+Separately, inspected libsignal `session_builder.js` invokes
+`verifySignature(..., true)`, whose `isInit` branch returns true without the
+primitive verification; that caller also ignores the result. This candidate
+does not fix that behavior. Signal's published [X3DH specification](https://signal.org/docs/specifications/x3dh/)
+describes verifying signed prekeys before continuing, but protocol-specific
+compatibility and the actual reachable impact here remain unverified. Neither
+finding is claimed to be the upstream GHSA or an assigned CVE.
+
+## 3. Image logging observation (separate)
+
+Local inspection of the exact pinned public image's config returned only safe
+booleans: its entrypoint invokes `deploy_database.sh` before `start:prod`.
+The image's `/evolution/Docker/scripts/deploy_database.sh` has SHA-256
+`29cf68a21b5c2220ed80eba47b83c5008a9cfe42b70a91a6ac0058e55ec90cc8`,
+contains an echo of `DATABASE_URL`, and invokes the database deployment script.
+No environment values or production logs were read. It is not established
+whether the variable was populated, printed in any actual run or exposed.
+
+This local candidate moves the integrity guard before the migration step and
+removes DATABASE_URL echo from both local build and deploy scripts. A synthetic
+entrypoint test uses fake migration/start commands and confirms an invalid guard
+prevents both. This does not repair the deployed image, investigate actual logs,
+rotate credentials, or authorize any production action.
+
+## Bounded next steps and decision required
+
+1. Owner decision: authorize any production Evolution security change after
+   review of the exact image and rollback plan. Isolated offline engineering
+   and the specifically approved test contact are already authorised; any
+   other real test identity needs its own authorisation. This security change
+   is separate from voice PRs #474/#26.
+2. Validate the supported replacement or reviewed fork with upstream maintainers;
+   examine the `isInit` behavior and fix/verify signed-prekey checks without
+   weakening transport. Record independent security review and dependencies.
+3. Produce a digest-pinned image with a guard *before* DB migrations, remove
+   secret-bearing output, prove runtime integrity independent of alternative
+   startup paths, inspect the full dependency graph, and verify a rollback plan.
+   Current candidate only checks selected files and npm startup path; direct
+   `node dist/main` and post-start mutation remain unguarded.
+4. In a separately authorised isolated environment, exercise startup, session
+   persistence, reconnect, direct/group inbound/outbound media and text,
+   webhook attribution, failure handling and migrations. Do not use live
+   client contacts or attack a production endpoint. Verify success/rollback
+   independently before proposing a production deploy.
+5. Any review of actual potentially secret-bearing logs or credential rotation
+   is a separate, narrowly scoped security action; do not infer a leak from
+   source or image inspection, and do not print credentials.
+
+The target image contains compiled JavaScript installed from npm: changing only
+Evolution's TypeScript source does not change the library wrapper. A maintained
+upstream fix or reviewed fork is preferable to local post-install patching. The
+current local candidate intentionally remains unreleased and non-production.
